    {
      "title": "Do not treat cash value as the entire death benefit",
      "text": "A $100,000 face amount does not mean $100,000 can be taken on surrender. Use the insurer’s current surrender quotation, its treatment of policy debt, additions and any applicable charges. Do not subtract an item twice if the quote is already net of that item.",
      "example": "A quote of $9,500 already net of a $1,500 loan still pays $9,500 under the stated facts.",
      "topics": [
        "5.4.01"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the actual table for extended-term duration",
      "text": "The common debt-free example keeps the original face amount temporarily, using available value to buy the stated term period. Age, available value, debt, additions and the applicable actuarial terms affect real values. The course’s eight years is an explicit hypothetical offer.",
      "example": "More years cannot be inferred merely because the original policy was called whole life.",
      "topics": [
        "5.4.02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Reduced paid-up is different from a paid-up addition",
      "text": "Reduced paid-up nonforfeiture continues a smaller permanent base benefit after the owner stops premiums. A paid-up addition is an extra amount purchased under the policy, often with dividends. Completion of a limited-pay schedule is another distinct way a policy can become paid up.",
      "example": "A reduction from $100,000 to $32,000 is not a new $32,000 addition on top of the original amount.",
      "topics": [
        "5.4.03"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Maryland’s statutory minimums are not promises of zero earlier value",
      "text": "Section 16-303(b) addresses a paid-up benefit after at least one year of premiums, with the specified request within sixty days of default. Its cash-surrender alternative after default distinguishes at least three years of premiums for ordinary life and five for industrial life. The statute permits corresponding provisions at least as favorable, and the contract may offer value earlier.",
      "example": "Read the actual table rather than announcing that every policy has no surrender value until its third anniversary.",
      "topics": [
        "5.4.01",
        "5.4.02",
        "5.4.03"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Default and payment timing have separate rules",
      "text": "Section 16-303(c) calls for the specified paid-up nonforfeiture benefit when another available option is not elected within sixty days after the defaulted premium’s due date. Section 16-304(b) reserves up to six months to defer cash-surrender payment after the stated demand and surrender. A cash surrender also defeats the standard §16-210 reinstatement provision.",
      "example": "An election window, a payment deferral and a reinstatement condition are different questions.",
      "topics": [
        "5.4.01",
        "5.4.02",
        "5.4.03"
      ]
    }
